  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM EDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\n- Dry With Smoke Today\n\nSurface high pressure and a dry atmosphere will keep conditions dry \ntoday as northerly post-cold frontal flow brings smoke back into the \narea. According to Michigan's Dept. of Environment, Great Lakes, and \nEnergy; The latest HRRR and RRFS smoke models show a band of smoke \nmoving southward through Southern Lower Michigan. This will create a \nperiod of air conditions that will be Unhealthy for Sensitive Group \nperiod tomorrow morning and afternoon. Latest model trends, along \nwith a pattern shift shows a reduction in smoke tomorrow evening. \n\n- Showers and Storms Monday Night into Tuesday\n\nStorms are expected to develop across the Upper Midwest Monday, \ndriven east by a shortwave and an attendant cold front. There are \nquestions given strong MLCIN as to the extent that these storms make \nit across the lake. Regardless, given the dynamics rainfall is \nlikely. One thing we will have to watch is if a MCS can develop \nalong the cold front. If it does, there is a risk of damaging winds \ngiven ample instability and shear. Thus the SPC's marginal risk \nacross the area.\n\n- Dry Conditions Tuesday Night into Late Week\n\nSurface ridging and a dry moisture profile then return post cold-\nfront Tuesday Night and continue through the rest of the work week. \nThis looks to keep conditions dry. Highs fall into the 70s mid week \nas 850mb temps fall into the single digits before climbing back into \nthe 80s by mid week.",
